const headings = [
  '1. Decide what a useful group means',
  '2. Let points choose representatives',
  '3. Make the geometry match the question',
  '4. Derive the updates and choose the seeds',
  '5. Implement a complete fit with an honest stopping rule',
  '6. Build a hierarchy one merge at a time',
  '7. Calculate Ward cost and read the height correctly',
  '8. Use library fits without losing the contract',
  '9. Choose a resolution and evaluate the result',
  '10. Turn clusters into a color palette',
  '11. Scale the computation and route a search',
  '12. Recognize when another objective is needed',
  '13. Practise, diagnose and transfer',
  '14. Complete a reproducible clustering report'
];
const headingId = heading => heading.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '-').replace(/^-|-$/g, '');

function Program({ example, children }) {
  return <section><Prose><strong>Before running:</strong> {example.question}</Prose><RunnableExample example={example}>{children}</RunnableExample></section>;
}

function Deeper({ title, children }) {
  return <details className="cluster-deeper"><summary>{title}</summary>{children}</details>;
}

function Practice({ title, question, hint, children }) {
  return <section className="cluster-practice"><H3>{title}</H3><Prose>{question}</Prose><details><summary>Get a hint</summary><Prose>{hint}</Prose></details><details><summary>Show the explained solution</summary>{children}</details></section>;
}

const kMeansHierarchicalContent = {
  title: 'K-Means & Hierarchical Clustering',
  readTime: '~95 min read + 2–3 hours practice',
  hasIntegratedGuide: true,
  content: () => <div className="lesson-pilot clustering-lesson">
    <LessonIntro prerequisites={<>Review <a href="/learn/path/full-curriculum/k-nearest-neighbors-knn?module=classical-ml">K-Nearest Neighbors</a> for vectors, distances and fitting transformations on training data. We refresh means and squared distance here. The complete Python programs use NumPy arrays with rows as observations and columns as features; the mathematical deeper branches are optional on a first pass.</>} sections={headings.map(heading => [headingId(heading), heading.replace(/^\d+\. /, '')])}>
      Learn to build groups when you have measurements but no supplied group labels. You will move representatives through a point cloud, construct and cut a merge tree, turn pixel colors into a palette, and decide what evidence supports a useful result. Start with the small examples, then use the complete programs and changed exercises to check whether you can explain the decisions yourself.
    </LessonIntro>

    <H2>{headings[0]}</H2>
    <Prose>A repair workshop has measurements from many machines but no reliable list of operating “types.” It wants a small set of representative conditions for inspection. One possibility is to group machines with similar temperature and vibration. Another is to group their entire time patterns. Those are different questions, even though both might be called <strong>clustering</strong>: forming groups from a chosen notion of similarity.</Prose>
    <Prose>Before choosing an algorithm, define one observation, the measurements available for it and the decision the groups will support. A row might represent a machine, a one-minute window, a customer or a pixel. A column might be a physical measurement, a count or a coordinate in a learned representation. Nearby rows are similar only in the representation and distance you chose. A neat picture does not establish a real-world category.</Prose>
    <LessonTable caption="Two ways to answer a grouping question" headers={['question', 'k-means', 'agglomerative hierarchical clustering']} rows={[
      ['What is built?', 'k representative vectors called centers or centroids, plus point assignments', 'A nested tree of merges, from individual points to one group'],
      ['What must be chosen?', 'Representation, k, initialization and fitting settings', 'Representation, distance/linkage and a rule for reading or cutting the tree'],
      ['Can a point change its group?', 'Yes, on the next assignment step', 'A completed merge is not undone in the same run'],
      ['How does a new row enter?', 'Transform it and assign it to its nearest fitted center', 'The fitted merge tree has no inherent new-row prediction rule']
    ]} />
    <Prose>The k in <strong>k-means</strong> counts representatives. In KNN it counted neighbors consulted for a prediction. K-means does not look up their supplied class labels; it learns centers from the feature vectors themselves. It still requires you to choose k. A full hierarchical tree postpones the flat-group count, but it does not remove choices about distance, linkage or which resolution is useful.</Prose>
    <Prose>For a shared small example, use six observations in a two-dimensional feature space. The coordinates below are constructed, not measurements from actual machines. P0 through P5 are observation IDs; a future cluster label is a separate identifier.</Prose>
    <LessonTable caption="The six points used in the center and merge investigations" headers={['observation', 'feature 1', 'feature 2']} rows={[["P0", 1, 0], ["P1", 1.5, .5], ["P2", 3, 2], ["P3", 3.5, 2], ["P4", 7, 5], ["P5", 7.5, 5.5]]} />
    <Prose>We will ask two things of these same points: which two centers represent them well, and what sequence of groups appears if we repeatedly merge? There need not be one answer that is best for every purpose. Two inspection teams may call for two representatives; exploring several resolutions may call for a hierarchy.</Prose>

    <H2>{headings[1]}</H2>
    <Prose>Start with two tentative centers at P0=(1,0) and P4=(7,5). For each point, calculate its squared distance to each center and choose the smaller. Squaring avoids a square root without changing which nonnegative distance is smallest. P2=(3,2) has squared distances (3−1)²+(2−0)²=8 and (3−7)²+(2−5)²=25, so it chooses the first center.</Prose>
    <Prose>After every point chooses, move each center to the coordinate-wise mean of its assigned points. The first four points choose the first center, whose new position is ((1+1.5+3+3.5)/4,(0+.5+2+2)/4)=(2.25,1.125). The other two produce (7.25,5.25). Repeat: new centers may change which choice is closest. This alternating process is <strong>Lloyd’s algorithm</strong>.</Prose>
    <Prose>The quantity being reduced is the sum of squared distances from each observation to its currently assigned center: <strong>squared error</strong>, <strong>within-cluster sum of squares</strong> or <strong>inertia</strong>. The initial assignment above costs 19.25. Moving to the two means reduces it to 7.6875. Those numbers describe the displayed centers and assignments together; a score from old centers cannot be attached to a picture of new ones.</Prose>
    <LloydLab />
    <Prose><strong>Predict before stepping:</strong> during assignment, centers stay fixed. During a mean update, memberships stay fixed. Watch which object moves at each phase. In the six-point separated start, the next assignment leaves these memberships unchanged. The fitted groups are therefore stable under these two operations; that alone does not prove they are the best groups for a business or scientific decision.</Prose>
    <MeanRepresentativeFigure />
    <Prose>The mean is a representative vector, not necessarily a real observed item. For values 1,2,9, the mean is 4 even though nobody supplied a 4. If the task requires selecting an existing photograph, document or machine as an exemplar, that constraint calls for a different representative, such as a medoid. We return to that choice later.</Prose>
    <Checkpoint prompt="With current centers at 0 and 6, where does the point 3 go? Would odd k solve this?">
      <Prose>It is equally distant from both. Specify a deterministic tie rule, such as choosing the lower-index center. Odd k does not prevent a nearest-center tie; that idea concerns only some binary voting ties in KNN. The choice of label at the boundary does not change this point’s current squared error.</Prose>
    </Checkpoint>

    <H2>{headings[2]}</H2>
    <Prose>Distance adds differences across columns, so units matter before any center is learned. Suppose two customers differ by 1,000 dollars and 1 visit. Raw squared distance is 1,000,001. Writing the same money difference in thousands of dollars makes it 1²+1²=2. No customer changed; the numerical meaning of “near” did.</Prose>
    <Prose>Choose a meaningful scale for each feature, then express a difference relative to that scale. If a difference of 1,000 dollars should matter as much as one visit, divide dollars by 1,000 before using Euclidean distance. Alternatively, state weights explicitly. A nonnegative weight w_r multiplies the squared difference in column r. To implement that weight by transforming coordinates, multiply the column by √w_r, not by w_r.</Prose>
    <MathBlock>{'\\begin{gathered}d_w(x,y)^2=\\sum_{r=1}^{d}w_r(x_r-y_r)^2,\\\\ z_r=\\sqrt{w_r}\\,x_r.\\end{gathered}'}</MathBlock>
    <FeatureGeometryLab />
    <Prose>Uniformly converting every coordinate from meters to centimeters multiplies all squared distances by 10,000 and leaves nearest-center choices unchanged when centers are converted too. Changing just one column’s scale changes the relative importance of that column. To preserve an existing distance when x_r becomes a·x_r, divide its squared-distance weight by a².</Prose>
    <Prose><strong>Standardization</strong> subtracts a training mean and divides by a training standard deviation. It makes a one-standard-deviation difference equally costly across columns. That can be sensible, but it is a modeling choice: a nearly constant noisy column or a rare binary flag can gain large influence. Centering alone does not change pairwise Euclidean distances. Scaling does. A constant feature contains no pairwise distance information and needs safe handling rather than division by zero.</Prose>
    <Prose>Missing values are not zero-valued observations by default. Decide how to measure, filter or impute them. Nominal category IDs such as red=1, blue=2, green=3 do not supply meaningful Euclidean intervals. Counts may benefit from a justified transform when relative differences matter. Write down these choices before looking for attractive clusters.</Prose>
    <H3>Run the examples in your own environment</H3>
    <Prose>The visual labs run on this page. The Python blocks are complete programs with recorded outputs; they do not execute in the browser. In a terminal with Python 3.12 available, create a project folder, change into it and run <Code>python -m venv .venv</Code>. Use <Code>py -3.12</Code> on Windows or <Code>python3.12</Code> on macOS/Linux instead of <Code>python</Code> if that is your installation’s command.</Prose>
    <Prose>Activate the environment with <Code>.venv\Scripts\activate.bat</Code> in Windows Command Prompt, or <Code>source .venv/bin/activate</Code> in a macOS/Linux shell. Then install the versions used for the recorded results:</Prose>
    <CodeBlock language="bash">{'python -m pip install numpy==2.3.5 scipy==1.18.1 scikit-learn==1.9.1'}</CodeBlock>
    <Prose>Save the next complete Python block as <Code>clustering_scaling.py</Code> in that folder and run <Code>python clustering_scaling.py</Code>. Save each later block in its own descriptively named file; it contains its own imports and inputs. If activation is unavailable, invoke the environment’s Python directly: <Code>.venv\Scripts\python.exe</Code> on Windows or <Code>.venv/bin/python</Code> on macOS/Linux, followed by <Code>-m pip install …</Code> or the script filename. Compare the printed result with the recorded output before changing a fixture.</Prose>
    <Program example={clusteringExamples.scaling}><Prose>Read the two comparisons separately: a pure change of units with compensating weights preserves a distance; a change of feature importance can change the partition. The held-out transformation uses scales learned on training rows. Unsupervised fitting can still leak information from a future evaluation population.</Prose></Program>

    <H2>{headings[3]}</H2>
    <H3>Write the two unknowns separately</H3>
    <Prose>Let X contain n row vectors x_i in d dimensions. Let μ_j be center j and a_i be the index assigned to row i. We choose k centers and assignments to make the following sum small. The norm squared means “sum of squared coordinate differences,” and J is the total error.</Prose>
    <MathBlock>{'\\begin{gathered}J(a,\\mu)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\|x_i-\\mu_{a_i}\\|^2,\\\\ a_i\\in\\{1,\\ldots,k\\}.\\end{gathered}'}</MathBlock>
    <Prose>For fixed centers, each row contributes its own term. Choosing a nearest center minimizes that row’s term independently. For fixed assignments, each center influences only its own group. The mean minimizes that group’s squared error. These two easy subproblems are why alternating updates are practical even though choosing the best partition jointly is difficult.</Prose>
    <MathBlock>{'\\begin{gathered}a_i\\leftarrow\\operatorname*{argmin}_{j}\\|x_i-\\mu_j\\|^2,\\\\ \\mu_j\\leftarrow\\frac{1}{|C_j|}\\sum_{i\\in C_j}x_i\\quad (|C_j|>0).\\end{gathered}'}</MathBlock>
    <Prose>C_j is the set of observation indices currently assigned to center j. The formula for a mean requires a nonempty group. Ties and empty groups need explicit rules in code; they are not details an implementation may ignore.</Prose>
    <Deeper title="Why the mean is optimal, and what weights change">
      <Prose>For a group C with mean m, write x−c=(x−m)+(m−c) and expand the square. Summing eliminates the cross term because Σ(x−m)=0. The remaining identity gives both the minimizer and exactly how much a misplaced center costs.</Prose>
      <MathBlock>{'\\begin{aligned}\\sum_{x\\in C}\\|x-c\\|^2&=\\sum_{x\\in C}\\|x-m\\|^2\\\\ &\\quad+|C|\\|m-c\\|^2.\\end{aligned}'}</MathBlock>
      <Prose>The second term is nonnegative and is zero exactly at c=m for a nonempty group. No calculus is needed. Differentiating gives the same answer: the gradient in c is 2|C|c−2Σx. With nonnegative observation weights v_i and positive total group weight, replace the mean by Σv_ix_i/Σv_i and the objective by Σv_i‖x_i−μ_a_i‖². A weight of three has the same objective contribution as three identical copies of a row. Observation weights and feature weights solve different problems.</Prose>
      <Prose>For fixed nonnegative feature weights shared by all centers, the coordinate-wise weighted metric still has the ordinary observation mean as an optimum in each positively weighted coordinate. A zero-weight coordinate does not affect the objective. It should not be interpreted as a learned discriminating feature.</Prose>
    </Deeper>
    <H3>Initialization changes the route through the objective</H3>
    <Prose>A good first center does not make the second one obvious. Randomly chosen rows can fall in the same region. <strong>K-means++</strong> spreads the starting opportunities by assigning each row a probability proportional to its current squared distance D_i² to its nearest selected center. First choose one row uniformly, then repeatedly sample from these probabilities and update the nearest-center distances.</Prose>
    <MathBlock>{'\\begin{gathered}P(\\text{next center}=x_i)=\\frac{D_i^2}{\\sum_r D_r^2},\\\\ D_i^2=\\min_{c\\in\\text{selected}}\\|x_i-c\\|^2.\\end{gathered}'}</MathBlock>
    <Prose>For one-dimensional rows 0,2,5 with 0 already selected, squared distances are 0,4,25. The next probabilities are 0,4/29,25/29. The farthest point is likely, but point 2 still has a chance. Choosing the farthest every time is a different rule. The lab’s probability strip shows exactly which interval of a uniform draw selects each row.</Prose>
    <SeedingLab />
    <Prose>When every row already coincides with a selected center, all D_i² are zero. There is no probability distribution to obtain by dividing by their sum. Extra centers cannot reduce the already-zero error. The seeding investigation exposes this stopping condition; the runnable implementation also shows how to honor a requested k by choosing an unused row, which can create a duplicate center and leave fewer than k occupied groups.</Prose>
    <Deeper title="What the k-means++ theorem does—and does not—guarantee">
      <Prose>For the original unweighted Euclidean D²-sampling algorithm, Arthur and Vassilvitskii prove E[J_seed]≤8(ln k+2)J*, where J* is the globally optimal k-center squared error and the expectation is over the random initialization. Exact non-increasing Lloyd updates cannot worsen that bound. It is an expected objective approximation, not a promise that every run recovers a particular latent class or places one center in every visually intended group.</Prose>
      <Prose>The original theorem is for vanilla D² sampling. Current scikit-learn KMeans uses a greedy k-means++ variant that considers several candidate centers per selection. Do not claim that merely sharing the name makes every implementation detail identical to the theorem. Multiple initializations compare candidate fits by the same objective; they improve the search opportunity without certifying the global optimum.</Prose>
    </Deeper>

    <H2>{headings[4]}</H2>
    <Prose>Every exact assignment and mean-update half-step is <em>non-increasing</em> in J. It may leave J unchanged because the best choice was already in place or a point is tied. Being bounded below by zero is not, by itself, a proof that an iterative algorithm stops: the positive sequence 1,1/2,1/4,… never reaches zero in a finite number of terms.</Prose>
    <Prose>For the finite-data Lloyd procedure, fixed tie and empty-center conventions make it possible to reason about stabilization of assignments and means. In exact arithmetic, with our deterministic nearest-center choice and retained empty centers, the process reaches a fixed assignment/update state. When a nonempty mean actually changes, the identity above gives a strict error decrease. There are only finitely many possible assignment-derived mean configurations. Floating-point implementations additionally need an iteration limit and, often, a tolerance.</Prose>
    <Prose>A stopped loop is not necessarily a fixed point. If an iteration limit interrupts the updates, reassignment to the returned centers may change labels. Those centers may then no longer equal the means of their returned groups. Report that status instead of printing “converged” because the loop ended. At an ordinary nondegenerate fixed point, Lloyd has made both conditional choices optimal; ties and empty centers complicate stronger local-optimum statements. There is no general global-optimum guarantee.</Prose>
    <Prose>The rectangle counterexample in the center investigation makes the distinction concrete. Four points (0,0),(0,1),(3,0),(3,1), with k=2, can stabilize as a bottom and top group. Their centers are (1.5,0) and (1.5,1), with total squared error 9. Left and right groups have centers (0,.5),(3,.5), with error 1. Both are fixed assignments under their own centers. Stability is therefore insufficient to establish the best objective value.</Prose>
    <Program example={clusteringExamples.lloyd}><Prose>The code validates a finite two-dimensional array, implements vanilla D² seeding and declares how ties and empty groups work. Its returned inertia is recalculated from its returned labels and centers. Check the deliberately short run and duplicate fixture as carefully as the favorable blob example. Matching a library’s error on one dataset is useful verification, not proof of global optimality.</Prose></Program>
    <Prose>The scratch program is for learning and small controlled checks. Production fitting needs optimized distance kernels, careful numerical behavior, stopping diagnostics and appropriate data validation. Do not replace a mature implementation with this one just because the sample output agrees.</Prose>

    <H2>{headings[5]}</H2>
    <Prose>Instead of moving centers and allowing memberships to change, begin with six singleton groups and repeatedly join two groups. After the first merge there are five groups; after five merges there is one. The whole merge history is a <strong>dendrogram</strong>, a tree whose leaves are observations and whose internal nodes are merges. Agglomerative means building upward by joining; divisive methods build downward by splitting.</Prose>
    <Prose>To choose the next merge, define how far apart two existing groups are. Four common linkage rules use the same input points but summarize their cross-group relationships differently. Ordinary Euclidean distance has the coordinate unit; squared error has its square.</Prose>
    <LessonTable caption="What a candidate pair of groups is scored by" headers={['linkage', 'score for A and B', 'what to watch for']} rows={[
      ['Single', 'Smallest distance between one member of A and one of B', 'A chain of nearby points can join groups whose endpoints are far apart'],
      ['Complete', 'Largest cross-group pair distance', 'Requires all cross-pairs to be close; an extreme point can delay a merge'],
      ['Average', 'Mean of all |A|·|B| cross-pair distances', 'Uses every cross-pair; it is not generally the distance between the two means'],
      ['Ward', 'Increase Δ in total within-group squared error after merging', 'Balances center separation with group sizes; requires Euclidean geometry for this interpretation']
    ]} />
    <LinkageDefinitionFigure />
    <Prose>For our six points, Ward first joins P2 and P3. They are 0.5 apart; their new mean is (3.25,2). P0/P1 and P4/P5 then form two more pairs. The resulting three groups look separate in this constructed example. A later merge records what this particular greedy procedure does next; it does not prove that nature supplied exactly three groups.</Prose>
    <Prose>The tree’s leaf labels use “observation · cluster”: P4·2 means observation P4 currently belongs to group 2. Group numbers only distinguish the current groups. They are neither quantities to multiply nor an ordering of quality.</Prose>
    <HierarchyLab />
    <Prose><strong>Read the axes before the colors.</strong> In the scatter view, horizontal and vertical position are feature coordinates. In the dendrogram, vertical height is the merge score under its stated convention. Horizontal leaf placement is chosen to draw the tree legibly. Rotating the two children of any branch changes the left-to-right order while preserving the hierarchy. Adjacent leaves are not necessarily each other’s nearest observations.</Prose>
    <Prose>A horizontal threshold includes merges at or below that height in this investigation. Branches connected below the line form a cluster. Alternatively, apply exactly a chosen number of recorded merges to obtain a chosen count. With equal merge heights, these are different policies: a threshold includes all tied merges together, while a merge-count prefix can stop partway through the tied operations.</Prose>
    <Prose>Our Ward tree has two successive merges at √.5≈.707107. Just below that height there are five groups; at that height both tied pairs are joined, leaving three. There is no horizontal threshold that produces exactly four groups on this tree. A four-group merge prefix depends on the declared order of the tied merges. Reporting the cut convention explains this apparent contradiction.</Prose>
    <Checkpoint prompt="If you rotate the left and right children of the final dendrogram node, do observations switch clusters at a fixed height?">
      <Prose>No. All ancestor relationships and merge heights remain the same. Only the drawing’s leaf order changes. A dendrogram is a record of the selected algorithm’s merges; it is not automatically a physical family tree, an evolutionary model or a causal explanation.</Prose>
    </Checkpoint>

    <H2>{headings[6]}</H2>
    <Prose>Ward asks a particularly useful question: how much extra squared error must we pay if A and B are represented by one mean instead of two? Let their sizes be a and b and their means m_A and m_B. The combined mean is (a m_A+b m_B)/(a+b). Moving A’s representative to that mean adds a‖m_A−m_AB‖²; moving B’s adds b‖m_B−m_AB‖². The within-group scatter already present stays in both sides of the comparison.</Prose>
    <MathBlock>{'\\begin{aligned}\\Delta&=\\operatorname{SSE}(A\\cup B)\\\\ &\\quad-\\operatorname{SSE}(A)-\\operatorname{SSE}(B),\\\\ &=\\frac{ab}{a+b}\\|m_A-m_B\\|^2.\\end{aligned}'}</MathBlock>
    <Prose>Two groups with the same center separation can have different Ward costs. Merging two singletons separated by 2 costs (1/2)·4=2. Merging two groups of 100 with that separation costs (10,000/200)·4=200. Ward penalizes giving many observations a worse representative; it is not simply nearest-centroid linkage.</Prose>
    <Callout title="A height convention that changes the numbers">
      SciPy’s Euclidean Ward linkage height is h=√(2Δ). Thus h²=2Δ and Δ=h²/2. The height has distance units; Δ has squared-distance units. For two singletons, h happens to equal their Euclidean distance. For larger groups it generally does not equal the distance between their means.
    </Callout>
    <LessonTable caption="Calculated Ward merges for the six-point fixture; ties use the stated stable merge order" headers={['new group', 'Δ squared error', 'h = √(2Δ)']} rows={[
      ['{P2,P3}', '.125', '.500000'],
      ['{P0,P1}', '.250', '.707107'],
      ['{P4,P5}', '.250', '.707107'],
      ['{P0,P1,P2,P3}', '7.0625', '3.758324'],
      ['all six points', '56.020833…', '10.585…']
    ]} />
    <Prose>Once the three pairs have formed, their internal squared errors sum to .125+.25+.25=.625. Merging the first two pairs adds 7.0625, giving 7.6875 for the two-group partition. Adding the final cost gives 63.708333… for the single group. Summing all Ward increments therefore recovers the total one-group scatter. This telescoping identity provides an independent check on a merge implementation.</Prose>
    <Deeper title="Derive the size factor and connect the tree to pairwise geometry">
      <Prose>Because m_A−m_AB=b(m_A−m_B)/(a+b) and m_B−m_AB=−a(m_A−m_B)/(a+b), the two movement penalties sum to [ab²+ba²]/(a+b)² times ‖m_A−m_B‖². Cancelling gives ab/(a+b). The same parallel-axis identity proved the k-means mean update: the methods share an error measure, while taking different routes through possible partitions.</Prose>
      <Prose>Greedily minimizing the next Ward increment does not minimize the total error for every eventual k. Earlier merges cannot be undone. Lloyd can reassign individual points; a Ward cut is constrained by its earlier joins. Nor does Ward guarantee spherical or equal-cardinality groups. Its score favors certain compact solutions in the chosen Euclidean geometry without enforcing those conditions.</Prose>
      <Prose>Single linkage has a different useful connection: at a distance threshold t, make a graph connecting observations whose distance is at most t. Its connected components are the single-linkage clusters at t. A minimum spanning tree preserves those threshold connections, so cutting its edges above t yields the same groups. This explains both efficient single-linkage constructions and the chaining effect: connectivity through a path does not require close endpoints.</Prose>
    </Deeper>
    <Program example={clusteringExamples.ward}><Prose>The scratch merge implementation calculates costs from actual group membership and compares the result to SciPy. The output distinguishes a linkage row’s children, height and merged observation count. Compare pair membership when a library uses different numeric labels, and inspect the at-most-k behavior of <Code>fcluster(..., criterion='maxclust')</Code> at tied heights.</Prose></Program>
    <Prose>A SciPy linkage input can be an n×d observation matrix or a one-dimensional <em>condensed</em> distance vector containing n(n−1)/2 distinct pair distances. A square n×n distance matrix is not silently equivalent: passed as observations, its rows would themselves become feature vectors. Use the documented conversion for precomputed distances. Ward must receive Euclidean geometry; already-squared distances are also not the correct distance input for its usual height convention.</Prose>

    <H2>{headings[7]}</H2>
    <Prose>Before running a library example, identify its input, learned state and prediction rule. KMeans fits centers and exposes training <Code>labels_</Code> and <Code>inertia_</Code>. <Code>predict</Code> assigns new transformed rows to centers. Cluster 0 is not “low,” cluster 1 is not “high,” and a large distance does not automatically produce a rejected or noise label.</Prose>
    <Prose>The native examples below were executed with Python 3.12.14, NumPy 2.3.5, SciPy 1.18.1 and scikit-learn 1.9.1. Set <Code>random_state</Code>, initialization and <Code>n_init</Code> explicitly, and record versions rather than expecting identical defaults forever. Small floating-point differences and tied label numbering can still vary across environments.</Prose>
    <Program example={clusteringExamples.production}><Prose>The 150-row blob generator is retained from the earlier lesson, but the outputs now come from executed fits. Its generating labels are available only because it is synthetic. Neither a high silhouette nor two implementations agreeing certifies a global optimum or validates a real deployment. These are accuracy examples, not measured speed benchmarks.</Prose></Program>
    <Prose>In the checked KMeans API, <Code>n_init='auto'</Code> uses one initialization for k-means++ and ten for random or callable initialization. Here explicit repeated starts make the experimental choice clear. KMeans chooses its best fit among runs. MiniBatchKMeans uses its initialization count to choose an initial configuration before one mini-batch optimization run; that is a different contract. Read the parameter documentation rather than transferring the interpretation by name.</Prose>
    <Prose>Library stopping may use small center movement, not exact assignment stabilization. The checked KMeans documentation explains that early stopping can leave centers inconsistent with the means of the final labels; it reassigns labels to agree with prediction on the returned centers. That is why the scratch example distinguishes a complete fixed point from a coherent but early-stopped return.</Prose>
    <Prose><Code>AgglomerativeClustering</Code> provides a flat partition for a selected cluster count or a distance threshold. For a complete inspectable tree, SciPy’s linkage matrix is convenient. Ward requires Euclidean distance. Single, complete and average can support other appropriate dissimilarities, but their results then answer those different distance questions. The standard agglomerative estimator has no <Code>predict</Code> method: attaching a new point to a nearest old center is an extra rule, not reconstruction of the original merge procedure.</Prose>

    <H2>{headings[8]}</H2>
    <Prose>More representatives can describe more detail. At the global optimum, allowing another center cannot increase the minimum squared error: the old solution can be retained while giving the extra center no useful work, or a group can be split. At one center per distinct observation, zero error is possible. Therefore minimizing training inertia across k without any cost for complexity does not select an interesting resolution.</Prose>
    <Prose>An <strong>elbow</strong> plot looks for a change in the rate of improvement, not a mathematically privileged bend guaranteed to exist. Separately fitted local solutions may even show a small increase as k grows because the optimizer found a worse candidate. Retry and inspect before treating that as a theorem-breaking property of the objective. A favorable initialization policy should be consistent across the comparison.</Prose>
    <Prose>The <strong>silhouette</strong> compares a point’s average distance to other members of its own group, a(i), with the smallest average distance to another group, b(i). For a nonsingleton group and a positive denominator, s(i)=(b(i)−a(i))/max(a(i),b(i)). A score near 1 means within-group neighbors are closer under this distance; near 0 means the averages are similar; a negative score means another group has the smaller average distance. It is not a measured probability that the point was misclassified.</Prose>
    <MathBlock>{'\\begin{gathered}s(i)=\\frac{b(i)-a(i)}{\\max(a(i),b(i))},\\\\ -1\\le s(i)\\le1.\\end{gathered}'}</MathBlock>
    <Prose>For a hand calculation, put 0 and 1 in group A and 4 and 6 in group B. For the point 1, its within-group mean distance is a=|1−0|=1. Its average distance to B is b=(|1−4|+|1−6|)/2=4. Thus its silhouette is (4−1)/4=.75. Notice that b uses an average over the other group, not just its closest point or its center.</Prose>
    <Prose>The usual implementation convention gives a singleton silhouette of zero. All-zero distances also need a declared safe convention. Scikit-learn’s overall score requires between 2 and n−1 distinct labels. Do not compare an undefined one-cluster or all-singleton score as if it were a numerical failure. A mean score can also hide one poor group, so inspect group sizes and individual silhouettes.</Prose>
    <ClusteringDiagnosticsFigure />
    <Prose>On this particular generated dataset, most of the displayed inertia reduction occurs from two centers to three, and the median silhouette peaks at three. Additional centers reduce the fitted error while dividing already compact regions. This is evidence about this fixture under this geometry and seed protocol; neither the visual bend nor the high score removes the need to define a useful grouping.</Prose>
    <LessonTable caption="Different kinds of evidence answer different questions" headers={['evidence', 'what it can establish', 'what it cannot establish alone']} rows={[
      ['Inertia / held-out distortion', 'How well centers represent rows in this geometry', 'Correct real-world categories or a best k without a complexity/utility decision'],
      ['Silhouette', 'Within-versus-between separation under the selected distance', 'Validity of a nonconvex, unequal-density or task-specific grouping'],
      ['Repeat seeds', 'Sensitivity to optimization initialization', 'Sensitivity to future populations or a flawed representation'],
      ['Resample rows / perturb features', 'Stability under a declared perturbation', 'Truth; a consistently irrelevant split can be stable'],
      ['Reference-label agreement', 'Agreement with that external partition, using label-invariant comparison', 'That its categories are the only useful target or were measured perfectly'],
      ['Domain and downstream checks', 'Whether the groups support the intended action on appropriate data', 'Causality or fairness merely from an appealing label']
    ]} />
    <Prose>Relabeling [0,0,1,1] as [7,7,3,3] leaves the partition unchanged. Comparing raw labels row by row would incorrectly call every row different. Compare which pairs share a group or use an appropriate permutation-invariant measure. Adjusted Rand index (ARI) accounts for chance agreement under its reference model; it is not a generic “accuracy” and may be negative. The later <a href="/learn/path/full-curriculum/clustering-evaluation-validation-silhouette-ari-nmi?module=classical-ml">Clustering Evaluation &amp; Validation</a> lesson develops these metrics and their assumptions.</Prose>
    <Prose>A sensible workflow chooses a small plausible range of k, compares repeated fits and profiles, checks sensitivity to justified representations, then freezes a decision for its intended evaluation. For future-row quantization, hold out rows and report average nearest-center squared error with the same training-owned transform. For an exploratory description of one fixed collection, fitting all rows can be appropriate; state that the result describes that collection instead of implying a generalization test was performed.</Prose>
    <Deeper title="Gap statistics and why stability still needs a question">
      <Prose>The gap statistic compares observed within-cluster dispersion with dispersion from simulated reference datasets intended to have no comparable cluster structure. One common form subtracts observed log dispersion from its expected log value under the reference. Its recommendation depends on the null generator, Monte Carlo variability and selection rule. A uniform reference in a bounding box is a modeling choice, not a universal definition of “no clusters.”</Prose>
      <Prose>For resampling stability, compare partitions on a common set of observations. A KMeans fit from each resample can predict a fixed probe set. A hierarchy needs an explicitly chosen comparison on shared sampled observations or another justified out-of-sample rule. Comparing unrelated bootstrap row positions compares different objects, even if both label vectors have the same length. Report the perturbation and reference set along with the score.</Prose>
    </Deeper>

    <H2>{headings[9]}</H2>
    <Prose>An image provides a concrete use for representatives. Treat each pixel’s three numerical color channels as a vector x_i=(R,G,B). Fit k centers, then replace each pixel by its assigned center’s color. The result is <strong>vector quantization</strong>: representing many possible vectors with a finite codebook. Here “cluster label” becomes a palette index, and squared error becomes a measurable reconstruction cost.</Prose>
    <PaletteLab />
    <Prose>The small mosaic is constructed in code so that its data, pixel counts and calculations are inspectable. Increasing k can preserve more color detail, but does not recover spatial boundaries by itself: the clustering sees colors, not pixel coordinates. Two distant pixels with identical RGB values have identical contributions. That is exactly what we want for a global color palette; it would be insufficient for segmenting connected objects.</Prose>
    <Prose>Repeated colors offer a useful exact reduction. If a color occurs 40 times, store it once with observation weight 40. Weighted squared error over unique colors equals squared error over every original pixel for the same centers. Unweighted unique-color clustering would instead make a once-occurring accent as influential as a dominant background. The weighted-mean identity explains why counts belong in the calculation.</Prose>
    <Program example={clusteringExamples.quantization}><Prose>Separate the floating-point centers from the byte-valued palette actually stored. Rounding channels can change both the reconstruction error and, after reassignment, the nearest palette choice. The output checks the equivalent weighted/repeated objective and reports the stored reconstruction instead of silently displaying a different palette from the one it scored.</Prose></Program>
    <Prose>For N pixels in a simple raw 8-bit RGB representation, the color payload is 24N bits. A toy indexed representation with k≤256 byte RGB palette entries takes 24k palette bits plus N⌈log₂k⌉ index bits, ignoring dimensions, headers, padding and coding details; k=1 can imply a single repeated palette entry with no per-pixel index bits under this toy convention. This is bookkeeping for a specified format, not a promise about PNG/JPEG file sizes. Existing compression can exploit spatial patterns and other structure.</Prose>
    <Prose>Squared distance in encoded sRGB channel values is a teaching objective, not a calibrated model of perceived color difference or linear light energy. A perceptually motivated color space changes the problem and has its own conversion/gamut details. Choose the metric for the visual task rather than assuming any three channels make equal differences equally visible.</Prose>
    <Prose>This connection explains a historical name: Lloyd’s classic paper studies least-squares quantization for pulse-code modulation. Representatives need not be categories with human names. They can be codebook values that trade storage or transmission resolution against distortion. The same center-assignment mechanism can also organize a search index, as the next section shows.</Prose>

    <H2>{headings[10]}</H2>
    <Prose>A direct dense Lloyd assignment compares n rows with k centers across d coordinates, costing O(nkd) work per iteration; mean accumulation adds O(nd). With I iterations and r complete starts, the main bound is O(rInkd), excluding initialization and input processing. These quantities matter more than a universal row-count cutoff. Chunking distance calculations avoids materializing every row-center-coordinate difference at once.</Prose>
    <Prose>Keeping the dense input costs O(nd) storage, centers O(kd) and labels O(n). A straightforward broadcasted NumPy demonstration may additionally materialize O(nkd) differences, while a chunked production implementation can avoid that temporary. Elkan-style k-means acceleration uses triangle-inequality bounds to skip some distances, but can require additional O(nk) memory; its benefit depends on the data and is not a change to the clustering objective.</Prose>
    <H3>Update representatives from small batches</H3>
    <Prose>Mini-batch k-means updates from a small subset rather than recomputing every full-data mean each step. The incremental-mean identity explains its basic update: if a center represents t assigned observations with mean m and a new assigned point is x, the combined mean is (tm+x)/(t+1)=m+(x−m)/(t+1). The new point’s influence shrinks as the count grows.</Prose>
    <MathBlock>{'m_{t+1}=m_t+\\frac{x-m_t}{t+1}.'}</MathBlock>
    <Prose>This running summary reflects assignments made while centers were changing; it is not generally the mean of a freshly reassigned full dataset. Individual mini-batch steps need not decrease full-data inertia. Repeated samples, initialization, reassignment rules and stopping settings matter. A count-based learning rate can also adapt slowly to genuine drift; forgetting old data is a separate modeling decision.</Prose>
    <H3>A merge tree can be expensive before it is drawn</H3>
    <Prose>There are n(n−1)/2 distinct pair distances. For n=10,000, storing just those values as float64 needs 399,960,000 bytes, about 381.4 MiB, before data and algorithm overhead. At n=100,000 it is about 40 billion bytes. A nice small dendrogram view does not reduce the computation needed to build a full dense hierarchy.</Prose>
    <Prose>The scratch Ward implementation repeatedly considers active pairs for clarity. SciPy’s documented implementations for single, complete, average and Ward use O(n²) time for the clustering stage and O(n²) memory, with method-specific algorithms; computing dense high-dimensional input distances adds its own work. Other linkage methods and implementations can have different bounds. A sparse connectivity constraint can reduce candidate merges but changes which merges are admissible, so it changes the problem as well as the resource use.</Prose>
    <Deeper title="BIRCH summaries preserve a useful identity, not every original partition">
      <Prose>BIRCH builds a compact tree of subcluster summaries. For a subcluster, store its count N, vector sum LS and sum of squared norms SS. Its mean is LS/N and its within-group squared error is SS−‖LS‖²/N. This follows by expanding every ‖x−mean‖²; the summary can be updated without retaining every row.</Prose>
      <Prose>If a later center c must represent that whole subcluster, its cost is the subcluster’s internal error plus N‖mean−c‖². Therefore weighted clustering of subcluster means reproduces the objective for that <em>restricted</em> assignment problem, up to a constant. It cannot recover a better original partition that would split a compressed subcluster. Also, the checked scikit-learn BIRCH global-clustering path does not automatically pass subcluster counts as sample weights: do not attribute this exact weighted objective to that API without implementing and verifying it.</Prose>
      <Prose>The identity has a numerical caveat: SS−‖LS‖²/N subtracts similar large quantities when data have a large offset and tiny variance. Stable centered or incremental calculations matter in floating point. Compression quality and geometric thresholds still require validation; a summary is not proof that no useful detail was lost.</Prose>
    </Deeper>
    <H3>Use a codebook to route a nearest-neighbor search</H3>
    <Prose>In an inverted-file vector index, first learn coarse centers. Assign each database vector to a center and store its ID in that center’s list. At query time, find a few close centers and search their lists. For a toy database split across four lists, probing one list can avoid examining the other three. It can also miss the true nearest vector near a list boundary.</Prose>
    <Prose>The cluster assignment is now a <em>routing</em> decision. More lists or fewer probed lists can change work and recall; they do not certify that the best neighbor is retained. Faiss’s IVF indexes implement this family of ideas. Measure recall against exact search on representative held-out queries and record index size and latency under actual conditions. A well-separated training codebook or low inertia alone does not establish retrieval quality.</Prose>

    <H2>{headings[11]}</H2>
    <Prose>Every point assigned to one of two distinct Euclidean centers is separated from the other center’s points by a straight equal-distance boundary. With more centers, each nearest-center region is an intersection of half-spaces: a convex Voronoi cell. That geometry helps with compact regions but cannot represent every intended shape.</Prose>
    <ClusteringShapeFigure />
    <Prose>A large, spread-out group may receive several centers while a small nearby group shares one. Unequal density, imbalance and outliers change squared error contributions; none is diagnosed solely by k. Because the loss is squared, changing an outlier’s distance from 10 to 100 multiplies its contribution from 100 to 10,000. K-means normally still assigns it to a center. A separate distance threshold needs its own data, operating point and validation before it becomes an anomaly detector.</Prose>
    <LessonTable caption="Change the objective when the needed notion of a group changes" headers={['need', 'candidate direction', 'qualification']} rows={[
      ['An observed representative under a chosen dissimilarity', 'k-medoids: minimize distances to selected observations', 'Less freedom than a mean; robustness and search cost depend on loss, data and algorithm'],
      ['Directions rather than vector magnitudes', 'Spherical k-means / normalized directional representations', 'Renormalizing centers is part of the directional objective; ordinary k-means on normalized rows is not generally identical'],
      ['Density-connected shapes and a noise label', 'DBSCAN / HDBSCAN', 'Density scale and sampling matter; these build different structures from a Ward tree'],
      ['Soft membership and ellipsoidal density components', 'Gaussian mixture model', 'Covariance, mixture weights and model checking matter; posterior responsibilities are not automatically calibrated real-world categories'],
      ['Neighborhood or graph structure', 'Spectral or kernel-based clustering', 'The affinity/kernel defines the geometry; dense matrices and an unsuitable similarity can create new problems'],
      ['A nested segmentation constrained by spatial adjacency', 'Connectivity-constrained agglomeration', 'Adjacency changes admissible merges; it does not prove semantic or causal validity']
    ]} />
    <Deeper title="Cosine geometry, kernels and the small-variance mixture connection">
      <Prose>For unit vectors x and y, ‖x−y‖²=2−2xᵀy. Nearest <em>unit</em> centers therefore maximize cosine similarity. But the mean of normalized rows need not have unit length. Ordinary k-means retains that mean, whereas spherical k-means renormalizes a nonzero group sum and uses a directional assignment objective. Zero vectors or cancelling group sums need explicit treatment. Normalizing the input alone does not make those procedures equivalent.</Prose>
      <Prose>Kernel k-means uses the same mean-squared-error idea after an implicit mapping φ. For a group C, distance to its feature-space mean expands to K(x,x)−(2/|C|)Σ_y∈C K(x,y)+(1/|C|²)Σ_y,z∈C K(y,z). A valid positive-semidefinite kernel makes this an inner-product geometry. The formula can model curved input-space boundaries without explicitly constructing φ, but pairwise kernel storage and its choice introduce real costs and assumptions.</Prose>
      <Prose>For an equal-weight Gaussian mixture with common covariance σ²I, responsibilities are proportional to exp(−‖x−μ_j‖²/(2σ²)). As σ² approaches zero, a uniquely nearest component receives limiting responsibility one, and the weighted mean update approaches the hard-assignment mean. Exact equal-distance ties do not automatically choose one component; finite-variance EM remains soft, and unequal priors shift its finite-variance comparisons. This limiting connection is useful, but ordinary k-means is not a general Gaussian mixture fit with learned covariances.</Prose>
    </Deeper>
    <Prose>In a high-dimensional dataset, simply adding many weak or noisy coordinates can dilute meaningful distance contrasts. Redundant columns can also count the same variation repeatedly. The effect depends on the distribution; there is no universal dimension at which Euclidean distance stops working. Feature selection, justified weighting and dimensionality reduction can help, but must preserve information relevant to the grouping question.</Prose>
    <Prose>For example, grouping patients by expression profiles requires a defensible measurement and normalization model, batch-effect checks and a clear decision about absolute levels versus profile shape. A dendrogram from 500 patients can be computationally manageable without being biologically valid. PCA does not guarantee that discarded low-variance directions were irrelevant, and UMAP/t-SNE visual islands are not independent confirmation of clusters. Compare meaningful external evidence without using it repeatedly to manufacture a favorable story. Any clinical claim requires validation beyond this algorithm lesson.</Prose>
    <Prose>The actual next module topic, <a href="/learn/path/full-curriculum/pca-dimensionality-reduction?module=classical-ml">PCA &amp; Dimensionality Reduction</a>, develops what a projection preserves and loses. Later <a href="/learn/path/full-curriculum/dbscan-density-based-clustering?module=classical-ml">DBSCAN &amp; Density-Based Clustering</a> and <a href="/learn/path/full-curriculum/gaussian-mixture-models-gmm-em-algorithm?module=classical-ml">Gaussian Mixture Models &amp; EM</a> give the alternative objectives their full treatment. These are connections, not a change to the module’s reading sequence.</Prose>

    <H2>{headings[12]}</H2>
    <Prose>Attempt each changed case before opening its hint. Explain the reasoning and convention, not just the final number. A useful readiness check is whether you can predict what a control will do without trying it first, then diagnose an unexpected result from the objective and inputs.</Prose>
    <Practice title="1. Move means on a new dataset" question="In one dimension, use rows 0,2,3,9 with k=2 and initial centers 0,3. Assign ties to the lower-index center. Calculate the first assignment, its error, the mean update and the next assignment. Is the first mean update already a fixed point?" hint="Compare squared distances for each row. Keep the assignments fixed while computing each new mean.">
      <Prose>The first groups are [0] and [2,3,9], with error 0+1+0+36=37. Their new means are 0 and 14/3. With these memberships the error is 86/3≈28.6667. The new midpoint is 7/3, so row 2 now chooses the first center. The first mean update was not a fixed point. The next groups [0,2] and [3,9] have means 1 and 6 and error 20. Another assignment moves row 3 left; eventually [0,2,3] has mean 5/3 and [9] has mean 9, with error 14/3. An apparently improved intermediate answer need not be finished.</Prose>
    </Practice>
    <Practice title="2. Prove why an extra center is not evidence" question="A colleague gets training inertia 2,500 for k=3 and 2,490 for k=4 and declares four real populations. What can you conclude? What if the fitted inertia for k=4 had been 2,510?" hint="Separate the globally best objective allowed by k, the result of this optimizer and the usefulness of a partition.">
      <Prose>The observed reduction is 10/2,500=.4%, but its practical meaning depends on units and the task. Lower error by itself cannot identify real populations. Inspect the range of k, fit stability, group profiles, the selected metric and an independent decision criterion. If a k=4 run returns 2,510, it can be a worse local fit or a changed setup; the globally optimal k=4 objective still cannot exceed the k=3 optimum. Do not infer a violation of that property from two imperfect optimization runs.</Prose>
    </Practice>
    <Practice title="3. Predict a seeding draw" question="Rows are 0,1,4, and the first center is 0. What are the vanilla D² probabilities? Which row is selected by a uniform draw u=.05 if intervals follow row order? What changes if all rows are 0?" hint="The cumulative interval for row 1 has length 1/17. Division by a zero total is not a probability rule.">
      <Prose>The probabilities are 0,1/17,16/17. Since .05&lt;1/17≈.0588, row 1 is selected even though 4 is farther away. With all rows at zero, every row is already represented exactly. Stop with zero error or use a declared duplicate-center fallback if an API must return k centers. Neither option justifies silently generating NaNs.</Prose>
    </Practice>
    <Practice title="4. Change the units without changing the question" question="A distance uses spend in dollars and a squared-distance weight of 1/1,000,000 on that column. You convert spend to thousands of dollars. What should the new weight be? Would independently standardizing every column preserve the same question?" hint="A coordinate multiplier a requires dividing its old weight by a².">
      <Prose>The coordinate multiplier is .001, so the new weight is (1/1,000,000)/(.001²)=1. This preserves that column’s distance contribution. Standardization instead uses data-dependent inverse-variance weights. It might be appropriate, but it need not preserve the original distance or objective. Explain why its relative weights serve the application.</Prose>
    </Practice>
    <Practice title="5. Derive a changed Ward merge" question="Group A has 3 observations with mean 1; group B has 2 with mean 6. Their internal squared errors are 2 and .5. Find the merged mean, the extra error, the new total error and the SciPy-style Ward height." hint="The means are five units apart. Keep the old scatter separate from the penalty for moving representatives.">
      <Prose>The mean is (3·1+2·6)/5=3. The increment is (3·2/5)·25=30. The merged error is 2+.5+30=32.5. The Ward height is √60≈7.74597, not 30 and not the centroid separation 5. Checking directly gives movement penalties 3·(1−3)²+2·(6−3)²=12+18=30.</Prose>
    </Practice>
    <Practice title="6. Read a tree with tied heights" question="A six-leaf tree has successive monotone merge heights .5,.7,.7,3,8. Under a cut that includes all merges at or below t, can any horizontal threshold produce exactly four groups? What do the two .7 operations imply for a four-group merge-count prefix?" hint="Start with six groups and count how many joins enter together at each distinct height.">
      <Prose>No. Below .5 there are six groups; from .5 to below .7 there are five; at .7 there are three. A merge-count prefix can stop after two merges and leave four groups, but it must choose which tied .7 merge was second. That is a tie-order decision, not a height difference represented in the data. Rotating the drawing does not resolve it.</Prose>
    </Practice>
    <Practice title="7. Diagnose a persuasive but misleading dashboard" question="A dashboard plots two UMAP islands, reports silhouette .82, names them low-risk and high-risk, and compares [0,0,1,1] with [1,1,0,0] as 0% agreement. Identify at least four separate issues." hint="Inspect geometry, the meaning of a metric, external labels and label permutation before looking at formatting.">
      <Prose>The projection can alter distances and create apparent separation; state whether silhouette uses original or projected coordinates and why. A favorable score does not prove the groups or validate a risk label. Such names need independent outcome evidence and an appropriate evaluation protocol. The two label vectors describe the same partition, so raw label accuracy is wrong. Also ask whether preprocessing and model selection used held-out information and whether the result is stable across justified representations and populations.</Prose>
    </Practice>
    <Practice title="8. Count colors, not just distinct color names" question="A grayscale image has nine pixels of value 0 and one of value 100. For a single unconstrained representative, compare the correct mean/error with clustering the two distinct values without weights. Why can the better floating-point mean still require a separate stored-palette check?" hint="The original image has ten observations. A repeated value must contribute repeatedly to the objective.">
      <Prose>The correct mean is 10 and error is 9·10²+90²=9,000. Treating 0 and 100 equally gives mean 50, whose error on the original ten pixels is 10·50²=25,000. Counts preserve the intended objective. In another example the learned mean may not be an integer channel value, so rounding or converting the stored palette can change the reconstruction and should be scored separately. Here 10 is already integral.</Prose>
    </Practice>
    <Practice title="9. Design a scientifically defensible grouping" question="You receive expression measurements for 500 patients across 20,000 genes and are asked for patient subgroups. Propose an analysis plan, then give one reason a technically correct Ward tree could still mislead." hint="Specify the observation and measurement model before choosing a normalization, feature reduction or clinical name.">
      <Prose>An acceptable plan states patient-level sampling, measurement/batch provenance, missingness and the intended use; chooses and justifies normalization and the meaning of similarity; checks sensitivity to feature selection and representation; explores suitable linkages/resolutions; and evaluates stability and external associations on appropriately separated evidence. Dense pair storage for 500 rows is only 124,750 values, about 1 MB of float64 distances, but the feature matrix and validation costs remain. A tree may primarily recover batch effects or irrelevant variation. Neither a high-variance projection nor a dendrogram establishes biological subtypes or clinical utility. There is no universal “use Ward, standardize everything and reduce to 50 dimensions” answer.</Prose>
    </Practice>

    <H2>{headings[13]}</H2>
    <Prose>The capstone treats clustering as a reproducible engineering decision: learn representatives on development rows, choose a resolution using development evidence, freeze the chosen transform and fit, then report distortion on untouched rows. The synthetic generator makes the experiment rerunnable; it does not establish performance on real machines or customers.</Prose>
    <Prose>The example declares its selection rule before evaluating the test set: each training group must have at least 20 rows and the median bootstrap ARI on common validation rows must be at least .8; among eligible k values, select the highest validation silhouette. These are illustrative operating requirements, not universal quality thresholds. Bootstrap means sampling training rows with replacement. Each refit predicts the same validation rows so the ARI compares the same objects. KMeans <Code>transform</Code> returns distances to its fitted centers; after the pipeline’s scaler, the smallest squared distance per row is the row’s representation error.</Prose>
    <Program example={clusteringExamples.capstone}><Prose>Check that no test rows are used to learn scales, choose k or select a seed. Read cluster sizes and center profiles alongside error. The label-invariant stability check compares predictions on the same development reference rows. The held-out result estimates only the stated representation task on this constructed sampling process.</Prose></Program>
    <Prose><strong>Your independent variation:</strong> change the data-generating seed, add a low-information feature, and compare one justified rescaling with the original representation. Choose all settings on the development portion again; then evaluate each frozen procedure once on its untouched test rows. Do not select the winner by repeatedly modifying settings after reading the test result. Include a one-center baseline for distortion and explain why its silhouette is undefined.</Prose>
    <LessonTable caption="What a complete submission should make inspectable" headers={['part', 'success check']} rows={[
      ['Task and representation', 'One row, feature meanings, transformations and the intended decision are explicit'],
      ['Fitting protocol', 'Splits, versions, seeds, initialization count, k candidates and stopping settings are reproducible'],
      ['Mechanism', 'A changed hand calculation agrees with code; tie/empty/early-stop behavior is explained'],
      ['Evidence', 'Distortion units, cluster sizes, per-group profiles and a label-invariant stability check are reported with their limits'],
      ['Hierarchy', 'Construct the six-point tree under another linkage and explain one changed merge and one valid cut'],
      ['Transfer', 'Explain why the added feature or rescaling changed—or did not change—the useful partition'],
      ['Limits', 'One counterexample and one alternative objective are justified; no claim of universal true k or biological meaning']
    ]} />
    <Prose>A strong explanation can say “these centers represent this distribution under these weights with this error; the grouping was stable under these changes, but a ring-shaped target would need another objective.” It does not have to pretend the experiment discovered the only possible categories. If you can derive an update, explain a tree height, reproduce a fit and diagnose a changed representation, you are ready for <a href="/learn/path/full-curriculum/pca-dimensionality-reduction?module=classical-ml">PCA &amp; Dimensionality Reduction</a>, the next topic in this module’s sequence.</Prose>
    <Sources alternatives={<><Prose>For a second explanation, use the annotated resources below after attempting the local examples. Older teaching material can be useful for the mathematics; use current documentation for API defaults and verify a video’s conventions against the calculations here.</Prose><ul>
      <li><a href="https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-071-the-analytics-edge-spring-2017/resources/video-5-hierarchical-clustering-0/">MIT OpenCourseWare: Video 5, Hierarchical Clustering</a> — a short narrated five-point merge/tree introduction, with an official transcript. The transcript was reviewed; its example uses centroid linkage, which differs from the Ward rule taught here. Treat its large-gap cut suggestion as a heuristic. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fuUC0WVeKsg">Watch on YouTube</a>.</li>
      <li><a href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/auto_examples/cluster/plot_kmeans_assumptions.html">Scikit-learn: Demonstration of k-means assumptions</a> — executable figures comparing anisotropy, unequal variance and uneven sizes; useful after the geometric counterexamples.</li>
      <li><a href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/auto_examples/cluster/plot_agglomerative_dendrogram.html">Scikit-learn: Plot Hierarchical Clustering Dendrogram</a> — a practical bridge from a fitted estimator’s merge structure to a tree; check its height convention.</li>
    </ul></>}>
      <li><a href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.cluster.KMeans.html">KMeans API</a> and <a href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.cluster.MiniBatchKMeans.html">MiniBatchKMeans API</a> — checked initialization, stopping, weighting and output contracts; see the versions recorded above.</li>
      <li><a href="https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.cluster.hierarchy.linkage.html">SciPy linkage</a>, <a href="https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.cluster.hierarchy.fcluster.html">flat cuts</a> and <a href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.cluster.AgglomerativeClustering.html">AgglomerativeClustering</a> — input shapes, linkage definitions, tie/cut semantics and algorithm-specific resource costs.</li>
      <li><a href="https://theory.stanford.edu/~sergei/papers/kMeansPP-soda.pdf">Arthur &amp; Vassilvitskii: k-means++</a> — the original D²-sampling algorithm and expected approximation theorem; advanced proof reading.</li>
      <li><a href="https://doi.org/10.1109/TIT.1982.1056489">Lloyd: Least Squares Quantization in PCM</a> — the classical representative/quantization connection; the paper’s setting is more specific than every modern clustering application.</li>
      <li><a href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/clustering.html">Scikit-learn clustering guide</a> — method comparisons, BIRCH summaries, mini-batches and diagnostics; compare the exact objective and API with the intended application.</li>
      <li><a href="https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss/wiki/Faiss-indexes">Faiss index descriptions</a> — inverted lists, coarse quantization and search controls; a deeper application of learned representatives to candidate routing.</li>
    </Sources>
  </div>
};

export default kMeansHierarchicalContent;
